.TH UCREF80 1 "November 2025" "um80 0.3.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ucref80 \- cross-reference utility for MACRO-80 assembly files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ucref80
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIoutput.txt\fR]
.I file.mac
[\fIfile.mac\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ucref80
writes a cross-reference listing for MACRO-80 (M80) assembly source files.
The listing shows where each symbol is defined and referenced.
The listing helps the reader to understand and maintain assembly code.
.PP
The cross-reference output includes:
.IP \(bu 2
Alphabetized list of all symbols
.IP \(bu 2
Definition locations (file and line number)
.IP \(bu 2
Reference locations (file and line number)
.IP \(bu 2
Symbol attributes (PUBLIC, EXTERNAL, MACRO)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v\fR, \fB\-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o " output.txt"
Write the cross-reference listing to the specified file
instead of standard output.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I file.mac
One or more assembly source files to analyze.
ucref80 processes multiple files together.
ucref80 tracks references across files.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
The output has one entry for each symbol:
.PP
.RS
.nf
SYMBOL_NAME
  Def:   filename.mac:line
  Refs:  filename.mac:line, filename.mac:line, ...
.fi
.RE
.PP
A special attribute follows the symbol name:
.IP \(bu 2
(PUBLIC) \- The symbol has a PUBLIC declaration
.IP \(bu 2
(EXTERNAL) \- The symbol has an EXTRN declaration
.IP \(bu 2
(MACRO) \- The symbol is a macro name
.SH SYMBOLS TRACKED
The cross-reference tracks:
.IP \(bu 2
Labels (lines that end with a colon)
.IP \(bu 2
EQU/SET/DEFL definitions
.IP \(bu 2
PUBLIC declarations
.IP \(bu 2
EXTRN declarations
.IP \(bu 2
Macro names
.IP \(bu 2
All symbol references in instructions and expressions
.PP
The listing excludes reserved words (instructions, directives and registers).
.PP
ucref80 reads a name as M80 reads a name:
.IP \(bu 2
A name has letters, digits and \fB$ . ? @ _\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A digit does not start a name.
.IP \(bu 2
A dot can begin a name or be inside a name (\fB.FOO\fR, \fBX.1\fR).
.PP
A dot-directive (\fB.Z80\fR, ...) is a reserved word.
If a colon follows the dot-directive at once, the word is a label.
A number, a string and a comment give no symbol.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Analyze a single file:
.B ucref80 program.mac
.TP
Analyze multiple files:
.B ucref80 main.mac io.mac util.mac
.TP
Write the listing to a file:
.B ucref80 \-o xref.txt *.mac
.TP
Analyze all .mac files:
.B ucref80 *.mac
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Analysis successful.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (for example, a file is not found).
.SH USE CASES
.IP \(bu 2
.B Finding unused symbols:
A symbol with no references may be dead code.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Impact analysis:
Before you change a symbol, find every place where the program uses the symbol.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Code documentation:
The cross-reference serves as a symbol index.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Debugging:
Make sure that the program defines and references the symbols correctly.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR um80 (1),
.BR ul80 (1),
.BR ulib80 (1),
.BR ud80 (1)
.PP
The original Microsoft CREF manual is in the retro_docs archive,
https://github.com/avwohl/retro_docs/blob/main/um80_and_friends/cref_lib.pdf
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
ucref80 processes only .MAC source files.
ucref80 does not process .REL object files.
.IP \(bu 2
ucref80 does not expand macros.
A reference inside a macro is a reference to a parameter name.
A reference inside a macro is not a reference to the expanded value.
.IP \(bu 2
ucref80 does not evaluate conditional assembly.
ucref80 scans all branches.
.SH AUTHOR
um80 project contributors.
